<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">London Conference of 1912–1913</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as London Ambassadors Conference · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Conference of 1912–1913 was a meeting of the ambassadors of the six great powers in London, chaired by Sir Edward Grey, to settle the outcome of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>. In July 1913 it recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> as an independent principality under great-power guarantee, but left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and other Albanian-inhabited lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. The powers then chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-prince-of-albania">Wilhelm of Wied</a> as Albania's prince.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london-conference-of-1912-1913.jpg" alt="London Conference of 1912–1913" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Sich at Serbian Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Potpisnici_londonskog_mira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The conference, also called the London Peace Conference or the Conference of the Ambassadors, brought together <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> after the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-league">Balkan League</a> had defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>. It was meant to arbitrate between the warring states over their territorial gains and to decide the future of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, whose independence had been proclaimed during the fighting. An armistice had been signed on 3 December 1912, and delegates of the Balkan allies, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, which had not signed it, and of the Ottoman Empire took part.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The conference met at St James's Palace. Its sessions from 16 December 1912 ended on 23 January 1913, when the Ottoman coup d'état known as the Raid on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sublime-porte">Sublime Porte</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-pasha">Enver Pasha</a> to power, and he withdrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from the talks. Without Ottoman delegates, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1915">Treaty of London</a> was signed on 30 May 1913, by which the Ottoman Empire gave up all its territory west of the Enos-Midia line.</p><p>On the Greek-Albanian frontier the powers intervened only once, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> forbade a Greek occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a> after the Greek navy shelled the town on 3 December; no power then opposed the cession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. The position of the Aromanians was also discussed. It was proposed, with the support of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, that the lands they inhabited around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindus">Pindus</a> go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> to shield them from Greek and Serbian assimilation, but in the end they were neither joined to Albania nor given autonomy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, about half the territory claimed by the new Albanian state, and between 30% and 40% of the total Albanian population, was left outside the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, above all the Kosovo Vilayet, which went to Serbia, and much of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chameria">Chameria</a>, which went to Greece. A boundary commission sent to draw the Greek-Albanian border could not do so on ethnographic grounds and relied instead on economic, strategic and geographical arguments, so most of the disputed area went to Albania. The local Greek population rose in revolt and declared the Autonomous Republic of Northern Epirus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “London Conference of 1912–1913” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its fate depended on the <b>Conference of Ambassadors</b> in London, which recognised Albania in 1913 but left Kosovo outside its borders.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-albania">Provisional Government of Albania</a></li></ul></section>
